(9)
zr(μ)(t)=D(μ)∑ini(μ,r)ϕi(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} z_{r}^{(\mu)}(t)=D^{(\mu)} \sum_{i} n_{i}^{(\mu, r)} \phi_{i}(t) \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(10)
x˙i(t)=−xi(t)+∑μ=1P∑r=1Rmi(μ,r)zr(μ)(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \dot{x}_{i}(t)=-x_{i}(t)+\sum_{\mu=1}^{P} \sum_{r=1}^{R} m_{i}^{(\mu, r)} z_{r}^{(\mu)}(t) . \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The magnitudes of these latent variables can be large O(N)\begin{document}$\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{N})$\end{document}, as in Fig. 2A) or small (𝒪 (1), as in Fig. 2E). We refer to any task whose latent variables are large as a “dominant” task, and the others as “non-dominant” tasks. This distinction is central to the mean-field description, because each neuron’s activity is significantly affected by any dominant task, meaning that a finite fraction of its variance can be explained by that task’s variables. This is not true for the non-dominant tasks, although they collectively provide non-negligible input as an ensemble. A task can be dominant for two reasons: 1) initial conditions or inputs pushing the state of the network to preferentially align with the task subspace, or 2) self-amplifying dynamics within the task subspace, as shown in Fig. 3. A random network state results in weak latent variables for every task.

We set aside a separate index µ∗ = 1∗, …, P∗, with P∗ ≥ 0 small, to enumerate tasks zr(μ∗)(t)\begin{document}$z_{r}^{\left(\mu^{*}\right)}(t)$\end{document} with dominant O(N)\begin{document}$\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{N})$\end{document} activity, if there are any. (Although in the main text we emphasized that there can be at most one dominant task, this is true for sufficiently long times (to forget initial conditions) and when there are no external inputs. Generally, initial conditions or external input drive can set multiple task-related latent variables to be large. Once inputs are released, the network eventually equilibrates to having at most one dominant task. In all cases, only finitely many tasks can be dominant since the state vector norm is at most N\begin{document}$\sqrt{N}$\end{document}.) Then µ = 1, …, P continues to enumerate the many non-dominant tasks with 𝒪 (1) activity. In the N, P → ∞ limit with fixed ratio α = P/N, fixed P∗, and fixed R, we can statistically describe the trajectories of all neurons and weak-task-related latent variables as decoupled processes, rather than as solutions to the high-dimensional dynamics of Eq. (1). In this mean-field description, the activity of the ith neuron xi(t) and the rth latent variable zr(μ)(t)\begin{document}$z_{r}^{(\mu)}(t)$\end{document} of the µth non-dominant task evolve as
(11)
x˙i(t)=−xi(t)+ηix(t)+∑μ∗=1∗P∗∑r=1Rmi(μ∗,r)zr(μ∗)(t)+∫−∞tdt′Gz(t,t′)ϕi(t′)ηix(t)∼GP(0,Qz(t,t′)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \dot{x}_{i}(t)=-x_{i}(t)+\eta_{i}^{x}(t)+\sum_{\mu^{*}=1^{*}}^{P^{*}} \sum_{r=1}^{R} m_{i}^{\left(\mu^{*}, r\right)} z_{r}^{\left(\mu^{*}\right)}(t)+\int_{-\infty}^{t} d t^{\prime} G^{z}\left(t, t^{\prime}\right) \phi_{i}\left(t^{\prime}\right) \quad \eta_{i}^{x}(t) \sim \mathcal{G} \mathcal{P}\left(0, Q^{z}\left(t, t^{\prime}\right)\right) \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(12)
zr(μ)(t)=ηr(μ)(t)+D(μ)∑r′=1RArr′(μ)∫−∞tdt′Sϕ(t,t′)zr′(μ)(t′)ηr(μ)(t)∼GP(0,(D(μ))2Cϕ(t,t′)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} z_{r}^{(\mu)}(t)=\eta_{r}^{(\mu)}(t)+D^{(\mu)} \sum_{r^{\prime}=1}^{R} A_{r r^{\prime}}^{(\mu)} \int_{-\infty}^{t} d t^{\prime} S^{\phi}\left(t, t^{\prime}\right) z_{r^{\prime}}^{(\mu)}\left(t^{\prime}\right) \quad \eta_{r}^{(\mu)}(t) \sim \mathcal{G} \mathcal{P}\left(0,\left(D^{(\mu)}\right)^{2} C^{\phi}\left(t, t^{\prime}\right)\right) \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
In deriving the theory, several functions of time emerge that are shared by all neurons and tasks: four kernel functions Cϕ(t, t′), Sϕ(t, t′), Qz(t, t′), Gz(t, t′) that describe effective noise statistics and interactions, plus the set of dominant-task latent variable trajectories {zr(μ∗)(t)}μ∗,r\begin{document}$\left\{z_{r}^{\left(\mu^{*}\right)}(t)\right\}_{\mu^{*}, r}$\end{document}. (In the main text, we wrote Sϕ(t, t′) = ⟨ϕ′(t)⟩ F(t, t′) and Gz(t, t′) = G(t, t′) to simplify notation—see section on self-consistency conditions.) All these functions’ values depend collectively on {(D(μ),A(μ))}μ=1P\begin{document}$\left\{\left(D^{(\mu)}, \mathbf{A}^{(\mu)}\right)\right\}_{\mu=1}^{P}$\end{document} and α, and they can be calculated by enforcing self-consistency, as we describe later.

If present, these symmetries will lead to effective self-coupling between each neuron and itself, whether through strong self-connections or multi-synaptic pathways. In a Neumann expansion of the the spontaneous state self-coupling kernel Gz(ω) (see section on self-consistency conditions),
(17)
Gz(ω)=α⟨DTr(A(I−DSϕ(ω)A)−1)⟩D,A \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} G^{z}(\omega)=\alpha\left\langle D \operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{A}\left(\mathbf{I}-D S^{\phi}(\omega) \mathbf{A}\right)^{-1}\right)\right\rangle_{D, \mathbf{A}} \tag{17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(18)
=α∑k=0∞(Sϕ(ω))k⟨Dk+1Tr(Ak+1)⟩D,A \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\alpha \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(S^{\phi}(\omega)\right)^{k}\left\langle D^{k+1} \operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{A}^{k+1}\right)\right\rangle_{D, \mathbf{A}} \tag{18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(19)
=α∑k=0∞(Sϕ(ω))kρk, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\alpha \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(S^{\phi}(\omega)\right)^{k} \rho_{k}, \tag{19} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which matches our intuition that neurons self-interact through strong network motifs, modulated by the appropriate power of the impulse-response function.

For the rest of the methods section and appendices, we often drop the neuron index i and non-dominant task index µ, because in the DMFT they are thought of as ensembles from which N and P samples are taken, respectively, rather than explicit lists of elements. A given non-dominant task µ has an associated set of D, A values that are sampled from a joint distribution Pr(D, A), and a given neuron i has an associated set of normalized (to 𝒪 (1)) couplings c={m¯(μ∗,r),n¯(μ∗,r)}μ∗r\begin{document}$c=\left\{\bar{m}^{\left(\mu^{*}, r\right)}, \bar{n}^{\left(\mu^{*}, r\right)}\right\}_{\mu^{*} r}$\end{document} to the dominant-task latent variables. We denote the random process for a single neuron with given couplings c as Wcx\begin{document}$W_{c}^{x}$\end{document}, and the random process for a single non-dominant task with given dynamics D, A as WD,Az\begin{document}$W_{D, \mathbf{A}}^{z}$\end{document}. The fully general set of self-consistency conditions are
(22)
Sϕ(t,t′)=⟨⟨δϕ(t)δI(t′)⟩Wcx⟩c \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} S^{\phi}\left(t, t^{\prime}\right)=\left\langle\left\langle\frac{\delta \phi(t)}{\delta I\left(t^{\prime}\right)}\right\rangle_{W_{c}^{x}}\right\rangle_{c} \tag{22} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(23)
Cϕ(t,t′)=⟨⟨ϕ(t)ϕ(t′)⟩Wcx⟩c \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C^{\phi}\left(t, t^{\prime}\right)=\left\langle\left\langle\phi(t) \phi\left(t^{\prime}\right)\right\rangle_{W_{c}^{x}}\right\rangle_{c} \tag{23} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(24)
Qz(t,t′)=α⟨⟨∑rzr(t)zr(t′)⟩WDz→,A⟩D,A \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} Q^{z}\left(t, t^{\prime}\right)=\alpha\left\langle\left\langle\sum_{r} z_{r}(t) z_{r}\left(t^{\prime}\right)\right\rangle_{W_{D}^{\vec{z}}, \mathbf{A}}\right\rangle_{D, \mathbf{A}} \tag{24} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(25)
Gz(t,t′)=α⟨D∑rr′Arr′T⟨δzr(t′)δIr′(t)⟩WD,Az→⟩D,A \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} G^{z}\left(t, t^{\prime}\right)=\alpha\left\langle D \sum_{r r^{\prime}} A_{r r^{\prime}}^{T}\left\langle\frac{\delta z_{r}\left(t^{\prime}\right)}{\delta I_{r^{\prime}}(t)}\right\rangle_{W_{D, \mathbf{A}}^{\vec{z}}}\right\rangle_{D, \mathbf{A}} \tag{25} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(26)
zr(μ∗)(t)=D(μ∗)⟨n¯(μ∗,r)⟨ϕ(t)⟩Wcx⟩c. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} z_{r}^{\left(\mu^{*}\right)}(t)=D^{\left(\mu^{*}\right)}\left\langle\bar{n}^{\left(\mu^{*}, r\right)}\langle\phi(t)\rangle_{W_{c}^{x}}\right\rangle_{c} . \tag{26} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Each kernel function (or order parameter) is computed as a within-process average parameterized by some value, e.g. ⟨⋅⟩Wcx\begin{document}$\langle\cdot\rangle_{W_{c}^{x}}$\end{document} or ⟨⋅⟩WD,Az→\begin{document}$\langle\cdot\rangle_{W_{D, \mathbf{A}}^{\vec{z}}}$\end{document}, that is itself averaged over the ensemble of all such parameters, e.g. ⟨·⟩c or ⟨·⟩D,A.

In the spontaneous state, the self-consistency conditions simplify tremendously. First, there are no zr(μ∗)(t)\begin{document}$z_{r}^{\left(\mu^{*}\right)}(t)$\end{document} values that need to be solved for, and thus the single-neuron dynamics simplify. Second, we can assume stationarity of the kernel functions, i.e. that each kernel function depends only on t − t′, not t and t′ separately. (This is a fairly mild additional assumption, but one that may be broken with sufficiently strong connectivity motifs [66]). Neurons are now statistically homogeneous, so we do not need to average over the ensemble of processes Wcx\begin{document}$W_{c}^{x}$\end{document} and can instead consider a single representative process Wx\begin{document}$W^{x}$\end{document}. The non-dominant-task latent variables are linear, and their associated autocovariance functions and impulse-response functions can be written compactly in Fourier space. With all of these simplifications, the self-consistency equations take the form
(27)
Cϕ(τ)=⟨ϕ(t)ϕ(t+τ)⟩WxSϕ(τ)=⟨δϕ(t)/δI(t−τ)⟩Wx \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C^{\phi}(\tau)=\langle\phi(t) \phi(t+\tau)\rangle_{W^{x}} \quad S^{\phi}(\tau)=\langle\delta \phi(t) / \delta I(t-\tau)\rangle_{W^{x}} \tag{27} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(28)
Qz(ω)=α⟨D2‖M(ω)‖F2⟩D,ACϕ(ω)Gz(ω)=α⟨DTr(AM(ω))⟩D,A, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} Q^{z}(\omega)=\alpha\left\langle D^{2}\|\mathbf{M}(\omega)\|_{\mathcal{F}}^{2}\right\rangle_{D, \mathbf{A}} C^{\phi}(\omega) \quad G^{z}(\omega)=\alpha\langle D \operatorname{Tr}(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{M}(\omega))\rangle_{D, \mathbf{A}}, \tag{28} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where M(ω) =(I − DSϕ(ω)A)−1 is the transfer function for a task with dynamics according to D, A.

