For notational simplicity, we assume there is one dominant oscillation, which we index by ∗. We first write down the dynamics for the two latent variables zr(∗)(t)\begin{document}$z_{r}^{(*)}(t)$\end{document} of the dominant pattern with pattern strength D(∗) and overlap A(∗), with intrinsic frequency ω(∗) = tan(θ(∗)). By the assumption of dominance, zr(∗)(t)∼O(N)\begin{document}$z_{r}^{(*)}(t) \sim \mathcal{O}(\sqrt{N})$\end{document}, we can divide by a factor of N\begin{document}$\sqrt{N}$\end{document}, making the 𝒪 (1) noise vanish for large N, and get
(32)
zr(∗)(t)N=D(∗)∑r′Arr′(∗)∫dt′Sϕ(t,t′)zr′(∗)(t′)N, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{z_{r}^{(*)}(t)}{\sqrt{N}}=D^{(*)} \sum_{r^{\prime}} A_{r r^{\prime}}^{(*)} \int d t^{\prime} S^{\phi}\left(t, t^{\prime}\right) \frac{z_{r^{\prime}}^{(*)}\left(t^{\prime}\right)}{\sqrt{N}}, \tag{32} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which reduces in the case of Gaussian x(t) to
(33)
(1+∂t)(zr(∗)(t)N)=D(∗)⟨ϕ′⟩∑r′Arr′(∗)(zr′(∗)(t)N), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left(1+\partial_{t}\right)\left(\frac{z_{r}^{(*)}(t)}{\sqrt{N}}\right)=D^{(*)}\left\langle\phi^{\prime}\right\rangle \sum_{r^{\prime}} A_{r r^{\prime}}^{(*)}\left(\frac{z_{r^{\prime}}^{(*)}(t)}{\sqrt{N}}\right), \tag{33} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
because Sϕ(t, t′) = Θ(t − t′) ⟨ϕ′(t)⟩ e−(t−t′). These dynamics are also consistent with the direct definition of the condensed patterns from the self-consistency conditions and applying Stein’s lemma to Eq. (26), which the Gaussianity of x(t) permits. Since ϕ is saturating, this system has a steady-state oscillatory solution of a particular amplitude ρ. Assuming marginal stability of the oscillation enforces constant ⟨ϕ′⟩ = 1/(γD(∗) cos(θ(∗)), which depends on both ρ and the noise variance—though we don’t yet know how this is partitioned between them. For now, we write the steady-state solution for the dominant pattern in terms of ρ as
(34)
z1(∗)(t)N=ρcos⁡(ω(∗)t)z2(∗)(t)N=ρsin⁡(ω(∗)t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{z_{1}^{(*)}(t)}{\sqrt{N}}=\rho \cos \left(\omega^{(*)} t\right) \quad \frac{z_{2}^{(*)}(t)}{\sqrt{N}}=\rho \sin \left(\omega^{(*)} t\right) . \tag{34} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The dynamics for any one neuron xm(t) features noise drive from ηx(t) sampled iid as a Gaussian process with stationary kernel Qz(τ), determined like before as an average over the 2-point functions of the weak-task trajectories, plus deterministic drive from this dominant task with coupling m(∗,r):
(35)
(1+∂t)xm(t)=ηx(t)+m(∗,1)Nρcos⁡(ω(∗)t)+m(∗,2)Nρsin⁡(ω(∗)t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left(1+\partial_{t}\right) x_{m}(t)=\eta^{x}(t)+m^{(*, 1)} \sqrt{N} \rho \cos \left(\omega^{(*)} t\right)+m^{(*, 2)} \sqrt{N} \rho \sin \left(\omega^{(*)} t\right) \tag{35} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(36)
=ηx(t)+m1ρcos⁡(ω(∗)t)+m2ρsin⁡(ω(∗)t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\eta^{x}(t)+m_{1} \rho \cos \left(\omega^{(*)} t\right)+m_{2} \rho \sin \left(\omega^{(*)} t\right) \tag{36} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where we have defined m(∗,r)N=mr∼O(1)\begin{document}$m^{(*, r)} \sqrt{N}=m_{r} \sim \mathcal{O}(1)$\end{document} as the order-1 set of couplings between this arbitrary unit and the condensed oscillation, which encode both amplitude and relative phase information. The P subscript in xm(t) indicates this dependence on m1,m2∼iidN(0,1)\begin{document}$m_{1}, m_{2} \stackrel{\text { iid }}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0,1)$\end{document}.

Any special case with Gaussian currents (e.g. the reservoir of oscillations) can take advantage of Gaussian integrals to simplify calculation. By our choice of nonlinearity ϕ(x)=erf(πx/2)\begin{document}$\phi(x)=\operatorname{erf}(\sqrt{\pi } x / 2)$\end{document}, we can leverage certain closed-form expressions:
(40)
⟨ϕ′(xi(t))⟩i=∫dy(2π)−1/2e−y2/2ϕ′(Cx(t,t)y)=1+(π/2)Cx(t,t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left\langle\phi^{\prime}\left(x_{i}(t)\right)\right\rangle_{i}=\int d y(2 \pi)^{-1 / 2} e^{-y^{2} / 2} \phi^{\prime}\left(\sqrt{C^{x}(t, t)} y\right)=\sqrt{1+(\pi / 2) C^{x}(t, t)} \tag{40} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(41)
ϕ(xi(t))ϕ(xi(t+τ)⟩i=2πsin−1⁡(Cx(t,t+τ)2/π+Cx(t,t)). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \phi\left(x_{i}(t)\right) \phi\left(x_{i}(t+\tau)\right\rangle_{i}=\frac{2}{\pi} \sin ^{-1}\left(\frac{C^{x}(t, t+\tau)}{2 / \pi+C^{x}(t, t)}\right) . \tag{41} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Whenever we consider Gaussian currents xi(t) in this work, they are always zero mean with possibly time-dependent variance Cx(t, t), but more often we are in the stationary case τ = t −t′ with Cx(0) = Cx(t, t).

The dimensionality calculation simplifies tremendously for the task-selected state. We can leverage the duality between neuron-neuron covariance (used most directly to define the PR) and time-time covariance (which has identical spectrum to the neuron-neuron covariance) as first shown in [45]. For a given variable a ∈ {x, ϕ}, its PR can be computed in terms of its autocovariance function Ca(τ) and four-point function Ψa(τ,τ′)=1N∑i,j=1NCija(τ)Cija(τ′)\begin{document}$\Psi^{a}\left(\tau, \tau^{\prime}\right)=\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i, j=1}^{N} C_{i j}^{a}(\tau) C_{i j}^{a}\left(\tau^{\prime}\right)$\end{document}

(42)

PRa=Ca(0)2Ψa(0,0).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{PR}^{a}=\frac{C^{a}(0)^{2}}{\Psi^{a}(0,0)} . \tag{42} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Leveraging the duality discussed above, we can alternatively calculate Ψa(τ, τ′) as

(43)

Ψa(τ,τ′)=N⟨Ca(t,t′)Ca(t+τ,t′+τ′)⟩t,t′.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Psi^{a}\left(\tau, \tau^{\prime}\right)=N\left\langle C^{a}\left(t, t^{\prime}\right) C^{a}\left(t+\tau, t^{\prime}+\tau^{\prime}\right)\right\rangle_{t, t^{\prime}} . \tag{43} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



For τ = τ′ = 0, we’re essentially computing the average square of Ca(t, t′) over the whole (t, t′) plane. The values near the t = t′ diagonal contribute negligibly compared to the whole plane, so we need only examine the asymptotic off-diagonal behavior when t−t′ is large. Any fluctuations δCa(t, t′) = Ca(t, t′)−Ca(t−t′) about the stationarized two-point function are negligible in this case, since typical values over the plane are 𝒪 (1) because of the dominant dynamics. Thus we can estimate Ψa(0, 0) directly from the autocovariance function Ca(τ) as

(44)

Ψa(0,0)=1T∫ττ+Tdτ′(Ca(τ′))2
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Psi^{a}(0,0)=\frac{1}{T} \int_{\tau}^{\tau+T} d \tau^{\prime}\left(C^{a}\left(\tau^{\prime}\right)\right)^{2} \tag{44} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



where the domain of integration depends on the nature of the dominant task. For a limit-cycle task, we need only integrate over one period of an oscillation to get the average behavior over the whole plane, as long as we start past when the amplitude decay is finished (Fig. S7). For a fixed point, we need only integrate over a brief interval when the Ca(τ) has reached its asymptotic value.

This section takes advantage of mathematical techniques which are outlined in e.g. [68] or [69]. Similar derivations can be found in [45]. In this section, we derive the DMFT for the spontaneous state, assuming no dominant patterns. We start by writing the Martin-Siggia-Rose-Janssen-de Dominicis path integral for the partition function Z(J) of the network dynamics:
(49)
Z(J)∝∫DxDx^exp⁡[i∑i∫dtx^i(t)(T[xi](t)−∑jJijϕj(t))] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} Z(\mathbf{J}) \propto \int \mathcal{D} \mathbf{x} \mathcal{D} \hat{\mathbf{x}} \exp \left[i \sum_{i} \int d t \hat{x}_{i}(t)\left(T\left[x_{i}\right](t)-\sum_{j} J_{i j} \phi_{j}(t)\right)\right] \tag{49} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
currently keeping the weights as shorthand Jij and using T = 1 + ∂t as notational convenience. We now compute the average
Z¯=∫∏μ,r,idmi(μ,r)∏μ,r,idni(μ,r)Pr({mi(μ,r)}r,μ,i,{ni(μ,r)}r,μ,i)Z(J) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \bar{Z}=\int \prod_{\mu, r, i} d m_{i}^{(\mu, r)} \prod_{\mu, r, i} d n_{i}^{(\mu, r)} \operatorname{Pr}\left(\left\{m_{i}^{(\mu, r)}\right\}_{r, \mu, i},\left\{n_{i}^{(\mu, r)}\right\}_{r, \mu, i}\right) Z(\mathbf{J})$$\end{document}
The probability density Pr(· · ·) for a given set of loadings (in general, vector-arrow notation imply a fixed-dimension vector like l→i(μ)∈R2R\begin{document}$\vec{l}_{i}^{(\mu)} \in \mathbb{R}^{2 R}$\end{document}, while bolded notation implies a vector with extensive (i.e. N-scaling) dimension, e.g. m(µ,r) ∈ ℝN) for each neuron i and each task µ,
l→i(μ)=(mi(μ,1),…,mi(μ,R),ni(μ,1),⋯ni(μ,R))∈ℝ2R \begin{document}$$\displaystyle {\overrightarrow{l}}_{i}^{(\mu)}=\left(m_{i}^{(\mu, 1)}, \ldots, m_{i}^{(\mu, R)}, n_{i}^{(\mu, 1)}, \cdots n_{i}^{(\mu, R)}\right) \in \mathbb{R}^{2 R}$$\end{document}
is
(50)
Pr({mi(μ,r)}r,μ,i,{ni(μ,r)}r,μ,i)∝exp⁡[−12∑i∑μl¯i(μ)TP(μ)l¯i(μ)], \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{Pr}\left(\left\{m_{i}^{(\mu, r)}\right\}_{r, \mu, i},\left\{n_{i}^{(\mu, r)}\right\}_{r, \mu, i}\right) \propto \exp \left[-\frac{1}{2} \sum_{i} \sum_{\mu} \bar{l}_{i}^{(\mu) T} \mathcal{P}^{(\mu)} \bar{l}_{i}^{(\mu)}\right], \tag{50} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
since each neuron’s loadings are independent of the others’, and loadings for different tasks are also independent. Within a task for a given neuron, the loadings are distributed as a multivariate Gaussian with precision matrix
P(μ)=inv(Σ(μ)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathcal{P}^{(\mu)}=\operatorname{inv}\left(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{(\mu)}\right)$$\end{document}
We define a set of R × P latent variables
zr(μ)(t)=D(μ)∑ini(μ,r)ϕi(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle z_{r}^{(\mu)}(t)=D^{(\mu)} \sum_{i} n_{i}^{(\mu, r)} \phi_{i}(t)$$\end{document}
and their definition-enforcing conjugates z^r(μ)(t)\begin{document}$\hat{z}_{r}^{(\mu)}(t)$\end{document}. Then our disorder-averaged partition function is
(51)
Z¯∝∫DxDx^DzDz^dl→exp[−12∑iμl→i(μ)TP(μ)l¯i(μ)+i∑μrz^r(μ)zr(μ)+i∑ix^iT[xi]−i∑iμrmi(μ,r)x^izr(μ)−i∑iμrD(μ)ni(μ,r)ϕiz^r(μ)]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \bar{Z} \propto \int \mathcal{D} \mathbf{x} \mathcal{D} \hat{\mathbf{x}} \mathcal{D} \mathbf{z} \mathcal{D} \hat{\mathbf{z}} d \overrightarrow{\mathbf{l}} \exp & {\left[-\frac{1}{2} \sum_{i \mu} \vec{l}_{i}^{(\mu) T} \mathcal{P}^{(\mu)} \bar{l}_{i}^{(\mu)}+i \sum_{\mu r} \hat{z}_{r}^{(\mu)} z_{r}^{(\mu)}+i \sum_{i} \hat{x}_{i} T\left[x_{i}\right]\right.} \\ & \left.-i \sum_{i \mu r} m_{i}^{(\mu, r)} \hat{x}_{i} z_{r}^{(\mu)}-i \sum_{i \mu r} D^{(\mu)} n_{i}^{(\mu, r)} \phi_{i} \hat{z}_{r}^{(\mu)}\right] . \tag{51} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where suppressed time indices imply time integrals over a shared dummy time index. We can rewrite this as a quadratic in terms of li(μ)\begin{document}$l_{i}^{(\mu)}$\end{document} by defining the vector
b→iμ=−i(x^iz1(μ),…,x^izR(μ),D(μ)ϕiz^1(μ),…,D(μ)ϕiz^R(μ))∈R2R \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \vec{b}_{i \mu}=-i\left(\hat{x}_{i} z_{1}^{(\mu)}, \ldots, \hat{x}_{i} z_{R}^{(\mu)}, D^{(\mu)} \phi_{i} \hat{z}_{1}^{(\mu)}, \ldots, D^{(\mu)} \phi_{i} \hat{z}_{R}^{(\mu)}\right) \in \mathbb{R}^{2 R}$$\end{document}
