Consider the following process for generating R×R matrices, where R must be even. We start by sampling R/2 independent 2 × 2 rotation matrices matrices via θl ∼ U(0, 2π) for l = 1, …, R/2:
(134)
Rl=(cos⁡θl−sin⁡θlsin⁡θlcos⁡θl), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathbf{R}_{l}=\left(\begin{array}{cc} \cos \theta_{l} & -\sin \theta_{l} \tag{134}\\ \sin \theta_{l} & \cos \theta_{l} \end{array}\right),$$\end{document}
and arrange them in a block-diagonal structure. We then take a random R × R Haar orthogonal matrix B and intermix the entries, but preserving the traces of all matrix powers. We finally scale the result by γ < 1 (e.g. γ = 0.99) to ensure that Σ=(IAATI)\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{\Sigma}=\left(\begin{array}{cc}\mathbf{I} & \mathbf{A} \\ \mathbf{A}^{T} & \mathbf{I}\end{array}\right)$\end{document} is positive definite. Then
(135)
A=γB(⨁l=1R/2Rl)BT \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{A}=\gamma \mathbf{B}\left(\bigoplus_{l=1}^{R / 2} \mathbf{R}_{l}\right) \mathbf{B}^{T} \tag{135} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
has a few desirable properties as a process for randomly generating a matrix. (For simplicity, the main text considers just R = 2, B = I =⇒ A = γR1, although its useful properties extend to this more general case.) First, for any k ≥ 1, we have
(136)
⟨Tr(Ak)⟩=⟨Tr(γk⨁l=1R/2Rlk)⟩=γk∑l=1R/2⟨Tr(Rlk)⟩=γk∑l=1R/2⟨Tr(Rl)⟩=0, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left\langle\operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{A}^{k}\right)\right\rangle=\left\langle\operatorname{Tr}\left(\gamma^{k} \bigoplus_{l=1}^{R / 2} \mathbf{R}_{l}^{k}\right)\right\rangle=\gamma^{k} \sum_{l=1}^{R / 2}\left\langle\operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{R}_{l}^{k}\right)\right\rangle=\gamma^{k} \sum_{l=1}^{R / 2}\left\langle\operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{R}_{l}\right)\right\rangle=0, \tag{136} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
using the fact that Rlk\begin{document}$\mathbf{R}_{l}^{k}$\end{document} has the same distribution as Rl. The γ factor conveniently also reduces the SEM when we effectively “measure” the mean trace matrix power for finite samples of such matrices in simulated networks, which can be large for large powers k.

For independently sampled D, this property eliminates the single-unit self-coupling by setting Gz(t, t′) = 0, but without resorting to a trivial distribution of A = 0 as is necessary and sufficient in the R = 1 case. We also have a closed-form equation for Qz(ω), which reads
(137)
Qz(ω)=α⟨D2‖M(ω)‖F2⟩D,ACϕ(ω). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} Q^{z}(\omega)=\alpha\left\langle D^{2}\|\mathbf{M}(\omega)\|_{\mathcal{F}}^{2}\right\rangle_{D, \mathbf{A}} C^{\phi}(\omega) . \tag{137} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where M(ω) = (I −DSϕ(ω)A)−1. Under these assumptions about the distribution of A, we can calculate Qz(ω). First, we note that the similarity transformation pops out of the entire M(ω) matrix:
(138)
M(ω)=(I−DSϕA)−1=(BBT−γDSϕB⨁l=1R/2RlBT)−1=(B(I−γDSϕ(ω)⨁l=1R/2Rl)BT)−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{M}(\omega)=\left(\mathbf{I}-D S^{\phi} \mathbf{A}\right)^{-1}=\left(\mathbf{B B}^{T}-\gamma D S^{\phi} \mathbf{B} \bigoplus_{l=1}^{R / 2} \mathbf{R}_{l} \mathbf{B}^{T}\right)^{-1}=\left(\mathbf{B}\left(\mathbf{I}-\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega) \bigoplus_{l=1}^{R / 2} \mathbf{R}_{l}\right) \mathbf{B}^{T}\right)^{-1} \tag{138} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(139)
=B(I−γDSϕ(ω)⨁l=1R/2Rl)−1BT⟹‖M(ω)‖F=‖(I−γDSϕ(ω)⨁l=1R/2Rl)−1‖F, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\mathbf{B}\left(\mathbf{I}-\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega) \bigoplus_{l=1}^{R / 2} \mathbf{R}_{l}\right)^{-1} \mathbf{B}^{T} \Longrightarrow\|\mathbf{M}(\omega)\|_{\mathcal{F}}=\left\|\left(\mathbf{I}-\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega) \bigoplus_{l=1}^{R / 2} \mathbf{R}_{l}\right)^{-1}\right\|_{\mathcal{F}}, \tag{139} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
since the Frobenius norm is invariant under orthogonal similarity transformations. Next we apply the matrix inversion on each block separately:
(140)
(I−γDSϕ(ω)⨁l=1R/2Rl)−1=⨁l=1R/2(I2×2−γDSϕ(ω)Rl)−1. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left(\mathbf{I}-\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega) \bigoplus_{l=1}^{R / 2} \mathbf{R}_{l}\right)^{-1}=\bigoplus_{l=1}^{R / 2}\left(\mathbf{I}_{2 \times 2}-\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega) \mathbf{R}_{l}\right)^{-1} . \tag{140} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Each component matrix that gets inverted I2×2 − γDSϕ(ω)Rl has eigenvalues 1−γDSϕ(ω)e±iθl\begin{document}$1-\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega) e^{ \pm i \theta_{l}}$\end{document}, so their inverses have eigenvalues 1/(1−γDSϕ(ω)e±iθl)\begin{document}$1 /\left(1-\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega) e^{ \pm i \theta_{l}}\right)$\end{document}. Since this matrix is clearly normal, its Frobenius norm squared is simply the sum of the eigenvalues’ squared norms. Putting it all together and assuming D is sampled independently from A, we have
(141)
⟨D2‖M(ω)‖F2⟩D,A=⟨∑l=1R/2(D2|1−γDSϕ(ω)eiθl|2+D2|1−γDSϕ(ω)e−iθl|2)⟩D,{θl} \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left\langle D^{2}\|\mathbf{M}(\omega)\|_{\mathcal{F}}^{2}\right\rangle_{D, \mathbf{A}}=\left\langle\sum_{l=1}^{R / 2}\left(\frac{D^{2}}{\left|1-\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega) e^{i \theta_{l}}\right|^{2}}+\frac{D^{2}}{\left|1-\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega) e^{-i \theta_{l}}\right|^{2}}\right)\right\rangle_{D,\left\{\theta_{l}\right\}} \tag{141} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(142)
=R⟨D2|1−γDSϕ(ω)eiθ|2⟩D,θ=R⟨D212π∫02πdθ1|1−γDSϕ(ω)eiθ|2⟩D \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =R\left\langle\frac{D^{2}}{\left|1-\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega) e^{i \theta}\right|^{2}}\right\rangle_{D, \theta}=R\left\langle D^{2} \frac{1}{2 \pi} \int_{0}^{2 \pi} d \theta \frac{1}{\left|1-\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega) e^{i \theta}\right|^{2}}\right\rangle_{D} \tag{142} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(143)
=R⟨D21−|γDSϕ(ω)|2⟩D. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =R\left\langle\frac{D^{2}}{1-\left|\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega)\right|^{2}}\right\rangle_{D} . \tag{143} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The last line requires assuming |γDSϕ(ω)| < 1, which is a fairly safe ass umption for a distribution of D with no outliers. Then, in Fourier space, the statistics of the effective neuron noise follow
(144)
Qz(ω)=αR⟨D21−|γDSϕ(ω)|2⟩DCϕ(ω). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} Q^{z}(\omega)=\alpha R\left\langle\frac{D^{2}}{1-\left|\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega)\right|^{2}}\right\rangle_{D} C^{\phi}(\omega) . \tag{144} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
With these simplifications, we have now a bipartite mean-field theory
(145)
(1+∂t)x(t)=ηx(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left(1+\partial_{t}\right) x(t)=\eta^{x}(t) \tag{145} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(146)
zr(t)=ηzr(t)+D⟨ϕ′⟩∑r′Arr′∫−∞tdt′e−(t−t′)zr′(t′) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} z_{r}(t)=\eta^{z_{r}}(t)+D\left\langle\phi^{\prime}\right\rangle \sum_{r^{\prime}} A_{r r^{\prime}} \int_{-\infty}^{t} d t^{\prime} e^{-\left(t-t^{\prime}\right)} z_{r^{\prime}}\left(t^{\prime}\right) \tag{146} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
because we know Sϕ(τ) = Θ(τ) ⟨ϕ′⟩ e−τ and Gz(τ) = 0. We can differentiate the second equation and get
(147)
(1+∂t)zr(t)=η˙zr(t)+ηzr(t)+D⟨ϕ′⟩∑r′Arr′zr′(t′), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left(1+\partial_{t}\right) z_{r}(t)=\dot{\eta}^{z_{r}}(t)+\eta^{z_{r}}(t)+D\left\langle\phi^{\prime}\right\rangle \sum_{r^{\prime}} A_{r r^{\prime}} z_{r^{\prime}}\left(t^{\prime}\right), \tag{147} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
exploiting the fact that the derivative of Sϕ(τ) is negative of itself, producing a leak term. With this representation of the weak-task latent variable dynamics, it is much more straightforward to determine the stability condition on D: it reduces to whether the largest real part among eigenvalues of D ⟨ϕ′⟩ A is below or above 1.

Let’s examine the single-site picture and solve for the temporal dynamics of the x(t) autocovariance:
(148)
(1+ω2)Cx(ω)=αR⟨D21−|γDSϕ(ω)|2⟩DCϕ(ω) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left(1+\omega^{2}\right) C^{x}(\omega)=\alpha R\left\langle\frac{D^{2}}{1-\left|\gamma D S^{\phi}(\omega)\right|^{2}}\right\rangle_{D} C^{\phi}(\omega) \tag{148} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(149)
⟹Cx(ω)=αR⟨D21−(γD⟨ϕ′⟩)2+ω2⟩DCϕ(ω). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Longrightarrow C^{x}(\omega)=\alpha R\left\langle\frac{D^{2}}{1-\left(\gamma D\left\langle\phi^{\prime}\right\rangle\right)^{2}+\omega^{2}}\right\rangle_{D} C^{\phi}(\omega) . \tag{149} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Now let’s make yet a further assumption, that D is in fact a constant such that geff2=αRD2\begin{document}$g_{\mathrm{eff}}^{2}=\alpha R D^{2}$\end{document} rather than following some distribution—this is a common choice for the non-selected tasks. Then we have
(150)
∂τ2Cx(τ)=(1−γ2ναR)Cx(τ)−geff2Cϕ(ω), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \partial_{\tau}^{2} C^{x}(\tau)=\left(1-\frac{\gamma^{2} \nu}{\alpha R}\right) C^{x}(\tau)-g_{\mathrm{eff}}^{2} C^{\phi}(\omega), \tag{150} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which is exactly the second-order ODE describing Cx(τ) for an unstructured network [36], except the Cx(τ) term is scaled by 1−γ2ναR\begin{document}$1-\frac{\gamma^{2} \nu}{\alpha R}$\end{document} of 1, where ν ≡ (geff ⟨ϕ′⟩)2 can be thought of as the effective slope for an equivalent linear network [43, 70].
